{ Repeat what you have learned. }
1. d4 { [%csl Rd4][%cal Rd2d4] } 1... d5 { [%csl Gd5][%cal Gd7d5] } 2. c4 { [%csl Rc4][%cal Rc2c4] } 2... c6 { [%csl Gc6][%cal Gc7c6] } 3. Nf3 { [%csl Rf3][%cal Rg1f3] } 3... Nf6 { [%csl Gf6][%cal Gg8f6] } 4. Nc3 { Ok, now we play a6. } { [%csl Rc3][%cal Rb1c3] } 4... a6 { [%csl Ga6][%cal Ga7a6] } 5. c5 { Develop your c8 bishop to f5.
5. e3 could also be played here. If you enter that variation, make pawn breaks with b5 and e5. } { [%csl Rc5,Bc8][%cal Rc4c5] } 5... Bf5 { [%csl Gf5][%cal Gc8f5] } 6. Bf4 { This is the Chameleon Variation of the Slav. Now, you can set up a London System as black and you have good fighting chances. These are four ways to play the Slav. There's one sideline I have to show you. } { [%csl Rf4][%cal Rc1f4] } *

